Of course!  In C, you can use loops to iterate over a sequence of elements, like a list of names or numbers.  The most common loop is the "for" loop.  Here’s an example:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    // do something
}

This will loop over all of the elements of the sequence "n" times, and for each of those elements the code inside the loop will be executed.  In this case, we’d want to do something like:

for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
    int x = i + 1;
    if (x % 2 == 0) {
        printf("%d is even\n", x);
    }
}

In this example, we’re checking if the integer "x" is even, which we can do by dividing it by two and checking if it’s divisible by two.  We then output some information about "x"; in this case, that "x" is even.

